Types of Finite Automata 1. Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) 2. Non-Deterministic Finite Automata (NFA) Difference between DFA and NFA Deterministic Finite Automata Non Deterministic Finite Automata From each state, for each symbol, there is exactly one transition From each state, for each input symbol, we can have 0 or more transitions No ε-transitions ε-transitions are allowed

Picturization of Finite Automata • A finite automata can be picturized as a finite control which is in some state from Q, reading a sequence of symbols from Σ written on a tape as shown below • In one move, the finite automata does the following:

1. Reads an input symbol 2. Moves its head one position to the right 3. Moves to a state as defined by δ

Deterministic Finite Automata The finite automata model that allows exactly one transition from a state on an input symbol is called Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) An input string is accepted by a DFA if it reaches one of the final states on reading the input string Example:

